{"qid":"Q965","names":["Burkina Faso"],"title":"Burkina Faso","endonym":{"native":"Burkina Faso","lang":"en"},"capsule":"Burkina Faso, known as Upper Volta (Haute-Volta) until the revolutionary renaming by Thomas Sankara on 4 August 1984, gained independence from France on 5 August 1960 as a landlocked Sahelian state in West Africa, successor to the French colonial territory that had incorporated the Mossi kingdoms. Sankara's government (1983–1987) implemented radical social programs—mass vaccination campaigns, tree-planting initiatives, women's emancipation—before his assassination in a coup led by Blaise Compaoré, who ruled until his removal by popular protest in 2014. The country experienced further coups in January and September 2022, with the junta led by Ibrahim Traoré withdrawing from French security arrangements and partnering with Russia's Wagner Group amid an escalating Sahel jihadist insurgency.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National borders are precisely attested by inherited colonial administrative boundaries and subsequent international agreements.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Burkina Faso","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Burkina-Faso"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Burkina Faso","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burkina_Faso"}],"britannica":"place/Burkina-Faso","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Burkina Faso","rulers":[{"qid":"Q529272","name":"Aboubacar Sangoulé Lamizana","start":1966,"end":1980,"image":"Sangoulé Lamizana, 1973.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sangoul%C3%A9%20Lamizana%2C%201973.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"